Hello 3D Printing Industry team,

a news tip about a software release that addresses a specific gap in the AM
workflow.

The gap: a downloaded STL contains triangles and no construction history. CAD
systems cannot edit it meaningfully, mesh tools can edit it but never return
exact surfaces, and anyone who needs a downloaded part to fit something has to
rebuild it. The two tools that came closest, Meshmixer and Microsoft's 3D
Builder, have both been discontinued, and parametric customisers on model
platforms only work when the original author anticipated the parameter.

Solidon3D 0.5.0 closes part of that gap. The application sits between the
downloaded model and the slicer: click a hole and its dimensions appear on the
model, type a new diameter and the countersink follows, pick a fit for the
screw, read the check report before export. As of this release those features
stay exact when they are edited, keeping real faces and edges rather than
converting to triangles, and STEP import and export are part of the round
trip. The options that used to expose the underlying kernel choice have been
removed; the software keeps the exact body where it can and names the limit
where it cannot.

For readers concerned with data integrity, two details matter. Every dimension
states its provenance in the feature window, so whether it was measured on the
mesh, fitted to a surface, or taken from a construction step. And a feature
keeps its identity through edits: closing a hole and drilling a new one
produces the same geometry but a different feature, and any fit pointing at it
loses its reference, while moving it keeps the reference.

One aspect sets the check report apart from what a slicer does: it names the
cause. Which island in which layer costs support material, and how far the
part would have to be turned to remove it, at a point where the part can still
be changed. Clearances follow the same idea, entered as a reference to the
material rather than as a number, with profiles that can be calibrated to a
specific printer from printed test pieces.

Also in this release: a honeycomb pattern or knurl is recognised as one
feature with pitch, cell width and depth instead of hundreds of faces;
dimensions are edited directly on the model; resin printers are supported; and
a model with open areas is closed while it is read instead of merely reported.

Solidon3D is an independent development from Germany, for Windows, macOS and
Linux, fully usable offline after a single activation, no account and no
telemetry. The AI features are optional and can run locally. Six languages,
and the demo is free and complete.

